1
00:00:04,849 --> 00:00:06,179
Twenty seven years in prison.

2
00:00:07,149 --> 00:00:08,239
Then one walk out.

3
00:00:08,756 --> 00:00:16,126
On 11 February 1990, Nelson Mandela walked out
of Victor Verster Prison, near Paarl, in South Africa.

4
00:00:16,684 --> 00:00:22,054
This is the story of what put him there, what
he refused, and what he said when he got out.

5
00:00:27,633 --> 00:00:34,543
On 21 March 1960, police killed sixty nine peaceful
protesters at Sharpeville, in South Africa.

6
00:00:35,052 --> 00:00:39,022
Eighteen days later, the government
banned the African National Congress,

7
00:00:39,672 --> 00:00:43,082
the ANC, which Mandela had joined in 1944.

8
00:00:43,943 --> 00:00:47,273
Mandela had been on trial for treason since 1956.

9
00:00:48,203 --> 00:00:51,933
In March 1961, he was acquitted,
and he went underground.

10
00:00:52,548 --> 00:00:55,418
In hiding, he became known as the Black Pimpernel.

11
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:58,370
He had argued for an armed wing of the ANC,

12
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:03,850
and it was formed, under the name
Umkhonto we Sizwe, Spear of the Nation.

13
00:01:04,449 --> 00:01:10,727
In 1993, the Nobel Committee said that he had first
been inspired by Gandhi's methods of nonviolence,

14
00:01:10,869 --> 00:01:13,379
but that the climate hardened after Sharpeville.

15
00:01:13,787 --> 00:01:18,877
In 1961, a film crew interviewed him,
and the year is burned into the picture.

16
00:01:19,527 --> 00:01:21,157
Listen to what he says about peace.

17
00:01:22,467 --> 00:01:29,187
There are many people who feel that it is useless
and futile for us to continue talking peace

18
00:01:29,207 --> 00:01:34,907
and non-violence against the government
whose reply is only savage attacks

19
00:01:34,927 --> 00:01:38,567
on an unarmed and defenseless people.

20
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:42,810
In January 1962, he left
the country for military training.

21
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:48,410
In July he came back, and on 5 August,
he was arrested at a roadblock near Howick.

22
00:01:48,813 --> 00:01:51,613
In November that year,
he was sentenced to five years,

23
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:54,263
for incitement and for leaving the country illegally.

24
00:01:54,926 --> 00:01:56,616
Then came a much bigger case.

25
00:01:57,326 --> 00:02:02,156
In October 1963, he stood in the Palace
of Justice in Pretoria with other accused,

26
00:02:02,746 --> 00:02:05,836
and the state alleged 199 acts of sabotage.

27
00:02:06,618 --> 00:02:10,868
The first indictment was thrown out,
and the accused were arrested again at once.

28
00:02:11,417 --> 00:02:17,447
On 20 April 1964, instead of giving evidence,
he made a long statement from the dock.

29
00:02:17,976 --> 00:02:22,786
He said that he had fought against white domination,
and that he had fought against black domination.

30
00:02:23,453 --> 00:02:27,923
He ended by saying that freedom was
an ideal he hoped to live for and to achieve,

31
00:02:28,653 --> 00:02:32,403
but that if needs be, it was an ideal
for which he was prepared to die.

32
00:02:32,955 --> 00:02:37,945
On 11 June 1964, the court convicted
all but one of the accused.

33
00:02:38,735 --> 00:02:42,265
The next day, Mandela and seven
others were sentenced to life in prison.

34
00:02:42,911 --> 00:02:47,841
He was sent to Robben Island,
and became prisoner number 466 of 1964.

35
00:02:53,915 --> 00:02:56,145
In 1968, his mother died.

36
00:02:56,755 --> 00:02:59,825
In 1969, his eldest son died in a car accident.

37
00:03:00,375 --> 00:03:02,045
He was forbidden to go to either funeral.

38
00:03:02,688 --> 00:03:07,778
In 1993, on American television,
he spoke about what it had cost his family.

39
00:03:08,488 --> 00:03:10,118
Here is part of that interview.

40
00:03:10,867 --> 00:03:15,127
And the fact that I was not there to protect my family,

41
00:03:15,727 --> 00:03:21,287
to guide my children, the terrible
part, a terrible pain, I did it.

42
00:03:21,407 --> 00:03:29,607
But after agonizing over this, in the end,
I nevertheless felt that I had done the right thing.

43
00:03:29,913 --> 00:03:34,203
In 1982, after nearly eighteen years on Robben Island,

44
00:03:34,753 --> 00:03:39,823
he was moved to Pollsmoor Prison,
as prisoner number 220 of 1982.

45
00:03:40,719 --> 00:03:45,549
The Nobel Foundation's own biography says
that his reputation grew steadily in prison,

46
00:03:46,099 --> 00:03:50,769
and that he consistently refused to compromise
his political position to obtain his freedom.

47
00:03:51,267 --> 00:03:57,107
In February 1985, President P. W. Botha offered
to free him and other political prisoners,

48
00:03:57,127 --> 00:03:58,657
if they renounced violence.

49
00:03:59,380 --> 00:04:00,490
Mandela refused.

50
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:04,690
His answer was read out at a rally
in Soweto by his daughter.

51
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:05,950
Here it is.

52
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:12,080
I have always been a member
of the African National Congress,

53
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:21,920
and I will remain a member of the African
National Congress until the day I die,

54
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:27,440
I cherish my own freedom dearly,
but I care even more for your freedom

55
00:04:27,676 --> 00:04:31,456
Later that year, after surgery,
he was held apart from his comrades,

56
00:04:31,476 --> 00:04:35,316
and began communicating
with the government about possible talks

57
00:04:35,336 --> 00:04:37,146
with the African National Congress.

58
00:04:37,767 --> 00:04:41,877
In 1986, he had his first meeting
with the Justice Minister, Kobie Coetsee.

59
00:04:42,167 --> 00:04:47,577
In 1988, he was treated for tuberculosis,
and in December he was moved to his last prison,

60
00:04:47,747 --> 00:04:52,217
Victor Verster, as prisoner number 1335 of 1988.

61
00:04:52,858 --> 00:04:56,108
In July 1989, he met P. W. Botha,

62
00:04:56,278 --> 00:05:00,468
the president who had offered him
conditional freedom, in his Cape Town office.

63
00:05:01,358 --> 00:05:06,488
In October, his old comrade Walter Sisulu
and four other prisoners were released.

64
00:05:07,088 --> 00:05:11,318
In December 1989, he met
the new president, F. W. de Klerk.

65
00:05:11,988 --> 00:05:13,478
On 2 February 1990,

66
00:05:14,048 --> 00:05:16,708
de Klerk told Parliament
that the African National Congress

67
00:05:16,728 --> 00:05:20,118
and all other political organisations
were no longer banned.

68
00:05:20,716 --> 00:05:23,326
Nine days later, Mandela would walk free.

69
00:05:28,941 --> 00:05:33,231
In the days before the release, Mandela
met de Klerk and was told the date.

70
00:05:33,801 --> 00:05:36,971
He objected to a plan to release him in Johannesburg.

71
00:05:37,321 --> 00:05:39,611
He wanted to walk out of Victor Verster.

72
00:05:40,104 --> 00:05:43,284
He also asked for more time,
so his supporters could prepare.

73
00:05:43,304 --> 00:05:47,214
De Klerk refused the delay,
but agreed to the prison gate.

74
00:05:47,790 --> 00:05:52,620
On 10 February, the president told
the press the date, the place and the hour.

75
00:05:53,105 --> 00:05:54,295
Here is that announcement.

76
00:05:55,133 --> 00:05:59,173
now in a position to announce that Mr.
Nelson Mandela will be released

77
00:05:59,193 --> 00:06:06,273
at the Victor Verster prison on Sunday
the 11th of February at about 3 p.m.

78
00:06:06,377 --> 00:06:11,619
He was due out at about three o'clock,
but he did not walk free until after four.

79
00:06:11,824 --> 00:06:16,094
He was seventy one years old, and few
people had seen a recent picture of him.

80
00:06:16,391 --> 00:06:20,001
He walked out beside his wife Winnie,
and the two raised their fists.

81
00:06:20,531 --> 00:06:23,781
His car was then trapped
in the crowds for about an hour.

82
00:06:24,163 --> 00:06:28,893
He reached Cape Town City Hall as night fell,
and spoke to the crowd from the balcony.

83
00:06:29,547 --> 00:06:32,397
He read his speech wearing
borrowed reading glasses.

84
00:06:33,083 --> 00:06:37,453
That night, he slept at Bishopscourt,
the home of Archbishop Desmond Tutu.

85
00:06:37,870 --> 00:06:42,060
Next day, a Cape Town newspaper
carried the headline free at last.

86
00:06:47,922 --> 00:06:50,192
The next morning, 12 February,

87
00:06:50,602 --> 00:06:54,272
he held his first international news
conference, with Winnie at his side.

88
00:06:54,727 --> 00:06:56,597
That day he flew to Johannesburg.

89
00:06:57,127 --> 00:07:01,697
The next, 13 February, he spoke
at a welcome rally at the stadium in Soweto,

90
00:07:02,267 --> 00:07:06,477
and slept that night in his family home
in Orlando West, for the first time in decades.

91
00:07:07,387 --> 00:07:09,517
He said he was overwhelmed by the welcome.

92
00:07:10,333 --> 00:07:13,973
I must confess, I am unable to describe my emotions.

93
00:07:15,413 --> 00:07:20,493
I was completely overwhelmed by the enthusiasm.

94
00:07:21,253 --> 00:07:23,493
It's something I did not expect.

95
00:07:23,665 --> 00:07:28,075
Asked about peace and the armed struggle,
he said there was no conflict between the two.

96
00:07:29,167 --> 00:07:34,707
his continuing commitment to armed struggle
and an equal commitment to peaceful solutions.

97
00:07:35,527 --> 00:07:39,987
There is no conflict between those two statements.

98
00:07:40,128 --> 00:07:46,948
In 1991, the ANC held its first national conference
inside South Africa since the ban of 1960,

99
00:07:46,968 --> 00:07:48,878
and elected him its president.

100
00:07:49,443 --> 00:07:53,993
The Nobel Committee said later that he went
straight from prison to the negotiating table.

101
00:07:54,477 --> 00:07:57,917
In September 1992, he and de Klerk signed a Record

102
00:07:57,937 --> 00:08:01,287
of Understanding on how a new
constitution would be written.

103
00:08:02,097 --> 00:08:06,107
In June 1993, the date of the first free election was set.

104
00:08:06,586 --> 00:08:07,776
It was not peaceful.

105
00:08:08,246 --> 00:08:11,146
In 1993, the Nobel Committee said that the number

106
00:08:11,166 --> 00:08:14,636
of people killed during the negotiations
was in the tens of thousands.

107
00:08:15,413 --> 00:08:20,083
In the 1993 American interview,
he also spoke about President de Klerk.

108
00:08:21,033 --> 00:08:28,113
There have been occasions when I did
not think that he lived up to expectations,

109
00:08:28,633 --> 00:08:33,553
but nevertheless destiny has put us in one country.

110
00:08:34,293 --> 00:08:43,633
And I cannot ignore him as the head of a government,
however discredited and illiterate it is.

111
00:08:43,733 --> 00:08:47,993
I must work with him in order
to build a new South Africa.

112
00:08:48,252 --> 00:08:53,122
In 1993, the Nobel Peace Prize
went to Mandela and to de Klerk,

113
00:08:53,212 --> 00:08:56,842
the president who had released him,
for their work to end apartheid peacefully.

114
00:08:57,535 --> 00:09:03,765
He accepted it in Oslo, on 10 December
1993, and paid tribute to de Klerk.

115
00:09:04,517 --> 00:09:07,067
Mr. F. W. de Klerk.

116
00:09:07,207 --> 00:09:14,767
He had the courage to admit
that a terrible wrong had been done

117
00:09:14,787 --> 00:09:20,867
to our country and people through
the imposition of the system of apartheid.

118
00:09:20,999 --> 00:09:26,049
In April 1994, South Africa held
its first election open to every race,

119
00:09:26,479 --> 00:09:28,389
and about twenty two million people voted.

120
00:09:28,887 --> 00:09:31,917
Mandela and the African National Congress won.

121
00:09:32,521 --> 00:09:37,751
On 10 May 1994, he was sworn in as
South Africa's first Black president.

122
00:09:38,269 --> 00:09:44,039
History.com reports that in his inaugural address he
said the time for the healing of wounds had come.

123
00:09:44,486 --> 00:09:49,656
In 1994, a Swedish journalist interviewed
him in the presidential palace in Pretoria.

124
00:09:50,246 --> 00:09:51,396
Here is part of it.

125
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:55,920
Oh, naturally, I was depressed several times,

126
00:09:56,200 --> 00:10:06,680
but the dominating feeling was one
of a conviction that whatever difficulties I have,

127
00:10:07,200 --> 00:10:13,840
I will return, and our struggle will triumph in the end.

128
00:10:14,043 --> 00:10:16,043
As president, he set up a Truth

129
00:10:16,063 --> 00:10:20,953
and Reconciliation Commission to investigate
human rights violations under apartheid,

130
00:10:21,723 --> 00:10:24,793
and in 1996 he presided over a new constitution.

131
00:10:25,938 --> 00:10:29,208
He retired in June 1999, at the age of eighty.

132
00:10:29,710 --> 00:10:33,892
Nelson Mandela died on 5
December 2013, aged ninety five.

